    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter today\u2019s slate with a lineup anchored by two premier power-speed threats facing Ranger Suarez. **Kyle Schwarber** (67.60) and **Trea Turner** (65.75) are our strongest plays, benefiting from a +7% park factor and the offensive upside of occupying the leadoff spot. While Suarez is a competent arm, the aggressive projections favor our elite hitters here, with **Schwarber** buoyed by a robust .401 xwOBA. We are also leaning heavily into **Yordan Alvarez** (69.33), who remains a must-start despite a matchup against Luis Castillo; his elite .421 xwOBA provides the floor necessary to overcome the SP's moderate strikeout ceiling. We are keeping a close eye on the lineups for **Turner**, **Schwarber**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Chase Meidroth**, **Caleb Durbin**, **Hyeseong Kim**, and **Pete Crow-Armstrong**; should any be bumped down the order, their efficiency scores will require immediate re-evaluation.\n\nThe decision to bench **Dillon Dingler** (33.13) was the most difficult call of the morning. While he possesses some underlying potential, a brutal matchup against the 2.93 xERA of McLean, coupled with a 15% penalty for platoon inefficiency, pushed him well below our 40-point efficiency floor. Conversely, we are forced to weather a significant risk with **Crow-Armstrong** (41.99). His score is heavily suppressed by a 19% penalty against Chris Sale and a 15% dynamic platoon disadvantage, making him a marginal play we are only rostering to keep the hope of a late-game steal alive. With **Junior Caminero**, **Christian Yelich**, and **Zac Veen** currently unavailable, the Zebras\u2019 depth is being tested, requiring us to extract every point of efficiency from the middle of the order, specifically looking for **Carson Benge** (62.86) to exploit his favorable matchup against K. Montero to keep our aggregate scoring competitive.",
